#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166639 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "954f8004-35b4-58ec-be26-2beccfd5f8e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "20b4b166-5dfe-5908-89b1-3f0a15afb59b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9306" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8a4c773c2ccc0bbab1ebb7126b781504549a46b6f5991753866eff98f3c8e03d.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = y^2(y-1)$\n\n- $y' = y(y-1)(y-4)$\n\n- $y' = -(y-1)^2(y-4)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/ee21aabb9f81aed281951e5ff9420c5f2985bfbc192791d8d2bbd1f1c87de156.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y = 1$ and $y = 4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation $y' = y^2(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n- The equation $y' = -y(y-1)(y-4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation $y' =-(y-1)^2(y-4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166640 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/id #uuid "9a55e013-edaa-5c6a-97af-cd42f94f6ed9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9307" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/998b30d8fb1df0c32a6ec63881ea247702a8e3888e7cd63ca371bc7dfe40ee2d.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y = -y^2(y+4)$\n\n- $y = y(y-4)^2$\n\n- $y = y(y+4)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/fb7c6c77767368871d3b39991093906f6a4a0f94e2a83075570e22b3f0e87a78.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-{y}^{2}(y+4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y <                                    -4$ | $-4 <                                    y <                                    0$ | $y > 0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny' = -y^2(y+4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y' = -y^2(y+4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166641 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/id #uuid "26be6eb9-9cd0-596d-aa0f-8912400a498f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9308" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/f93764bdd958d41e90e09e8be2f52754835c67bc5a2ff9f6186454ebbd245232.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$\n\n- $y'= \\dfrac1{y^2+y}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8820a0d0a4e0fc9d0089dea3c34a67bfaf619edcb7e0552d14a11b42a4cd20e5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{y+1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(1+\\dfrac1y\\right)}\\to 1 \\quad\\text{as} \\quad y\\to \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac1{y^2+y}= \\dfrac1{y(y+1)}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical for *both* $y \\to -1$ *and* $y \\to 0,$ \nwhich is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that $y'\\to 0$ as $y\\to\\pm\\infty,$ and $y'\\to \\infty$ as $y\\to -1.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166643 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "f128f444-1d32-5343-a729-963030131c76" :question/math-academy-id "e-9314" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/b064d9170c89eda881a1247b14fa295bae6b78d794ad7737aa86872fb5277af1.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y$\n\n- $y' = y^2 - 2y$\n\n- $y' = y^3 + y^2$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/96fa01db29c0effec6c81996cfcaad1d46ffa71f2f1b2b3da0a48dc39bfb0d26.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\ny'= y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y = y(y^2 - 4y + 4) = y(y-2)^2\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\ny'=  y^2 - 2y = y(y-2).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y <        0$ | $0 <          y <          2$ | $y > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny ' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y =y(y-2)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y' =y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c128a86e-d78e-5baa-b737-36cd9a6d2ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} =-\\frac{1}{2}y(y + 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163509} {:answer/id #uuid "26f0c3c5-092f-510c-8fa1-30a9171632de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\frac{1}{2}y^{2}(y + 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163510} {:answer/id #uuid "369a5254-639f-5506-bdb5-79a54221cefd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\frac{1}{2}y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163511} {:answer/id #uuid "395d94d3-6382-5b46-8e79-8d780cb8ae06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\frac{1}{2}y(y + 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163512} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8b04e7-6448-5bb0-90b4-b3b9b2908fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} =-\\frac{1}{2}(y + 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c128a86e-d78e-5baa-b737-36cd9a6d2ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} =-\\frac{1}{2}y(y + 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163509} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fa0a6ee-3caa-5179-b82c-b58cb6511513" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45ddced2-4265-581a-ad59-d353bca932c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-115466" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/b7feb77818b19ab8b61471df9057a15fb3c39d6babc4aeee56a73e1d3c3756cb.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/1e37d101f131279fc198fd97f03e6a35e15bc2d62622e1730aadbc316dc8d0bf.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}(y+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c2c6a81-3f03-541a-9325-21f5f0c5abf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163521} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2a884b-11ee-57b3-9e7c-6ad6ec23142f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163522} {:answer/id #uuid "e07814c4-1d3f-5ce3-ad92-37639e80d539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163523} {:answer/id #uuid "302b2a05-5076-5cad-8abe-249eefc23414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163524} {:answer/id #uuid "4334b047-6d7a-580d-951e-2708af4e7512" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc2a884b-11ee-57b3-9e7c-6ad6ec23142f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163522} :answer-field/id #uuid "1083c99a-8c83-5801-97ba-64a13cca592d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "85b66c06-8b49-5d46-861f-2424f521305f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115479" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7bb52825944a7515a6281a88b5bc740cf6d097380fa13fe7e00efc5e499d49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y<0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for all $y\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "954f8004-35b4-58ec-be26-2beccfd5f8e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8e6f230-2c29-5579-86d3-60ce540b7465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312190} {:answer/id #uuid "feccdb42-0fe5-5c9c-a4a2-6760c6afbef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312191} {:answer/id #uuid "dca62aa0-84de-553f-a9db-dab5f8677a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312192} {:answer/id #uuid "a830f33f-40f9-5a62-9912-1ef005ae4ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312193} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba2c7d0-a506-5798-adc3-71fdb7d8e1a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba2c7d0-a506-5798-adc3-71fdb7d8e1a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312194} :answer-field/id #uuid "d41ad310-ab0d-515c-8afc-c90bf4ea5370" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7474d0ef-0afb-5a88-8581-bff8157f9b7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115876" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/bedcab6fac21c2c178604e94f04f31bf1f423562fd653fd18ae5e0ea949e38dc.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(y-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/84b459d5682327d618116cd5d3ef331263548c754d2d8e0759e6fcb084fd466c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=-4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+2)(y+4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=-4,$ and $a+b=-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "954f8004-35b4-58ec-be26-2beccfd5f8e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "753cdb5a-4ee8-5337-a4d2-d4d47de5ca33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312197} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fa176e-e7b1-54ef-8d20-58f23d7c2046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312198} {:answer/id #uuid "8caa5386-a39e-541b-81af-d68ba86a614f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312199} {:answer/id #uuid "60e07d00-ce67-5a79-852c-f5183f21fe6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312200} {:answer/id #uuid "c73878a6-febf-52be-b384-bb72d3c685e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8caa5386-a39e-541b-81af-d68ba86a614f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312199} :answer-field/id #uuid "85f09089-2328-5171-87e2-062d32b0c0e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4f07403-165c-563e-b91e-76206e676d8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115880" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/bc5b29fca0521e3da39993d0359e857333611c68c35e49c037ca7af031288ba3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+4)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y+4)$\n- ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)(y+4)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/cd73bc9f35e167537996a3b33b84471dcb8fefe2eccb4afd21ca55a23e6478fd.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y+4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)(y+4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=-4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=-1,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "954f8004-35b4-58ec-be26-2beccfd5f8e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15e7d479-5bee-5068-a8cd-0a5f6cbc2d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312204} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf147bd-8efb-5e25-884a-946ae2acbd6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312205} {:answer/id #uuid "8effa258-b99d-5eea-8e6f-5c67e1051d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312206} {:answer/id #uuid "28f361e7-2329-5da8-af60-0e78cc1948f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312207} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba4600f-9dec-53cc-a808-ea0bc1a9d7c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cf147bd-8efb-5e25-884a-946ae2acbd6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312205} :answer-field/id #uuid "a831e90e-bf4c-5bdf-b54a-8af6adc87a44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81165700-39d9-5e62-91be-1dd6097a0758" :question/math-academy-id "q-115888" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/2e2d4b78d8181efc77f65af0c3dc4b3110d0203e650006a0e99d9a8ba933b53f.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-(y-1)(y-4)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)(y-4)$\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y-1)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7e207bc990a9dfbafa8474ad13c17482e21c323833be848f4ebbaa580abeb201.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=1$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-(y-1)(y-4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)(y-4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b74286d9-ef23-5923-9e17-115ebf9afdf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312211} {:answer/id #uuid "0904ecf8-e274-57e8-a6ed-ef842d7fced8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312212} {:answer/id #uuid "5270eac7-d57e-54a0-a8b5-cdc517b67383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312213} {:answer/id #uuid "b7edd58f-d5f6-53ff-a7a8-54cdf7956e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312214} {:answer/id #uuid "40807088-e812-53d8-9ccc-c815156e772d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40807088-e812-53d8-9ccc-c815156e772d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312215} :answer-field/id #uuid "f94163e5-5a0e-5e62-b4b0-4414a42a2e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af0eae53-a90a-516b-8984-504779f551ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-115935" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/57ecd122ba41b296b3884ecd4f748f6b4ac4bd1d26f14d7b5166c5fe6e33674b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(1-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/e109d237419aaba5efcc0c65c2d97c8f8ded17d18f98bb0a95eba3336d224590.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=\\pm 1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(1-y)$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077f0dac-5374-5a0b-aff6-e6d58343cb4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312218} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6000b8-ce60-57f1-b233-3244b9f8994e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312219} {:answer/id #uuid "06215df3-fb34-51b4-87e2-a154e3b3abbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312220} {:answer/id #uuid "1c0ee75c-43cd-56f1-bd82-42c28885096a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312221} {:answer/id #uuid "65d43874-65f4-578d-9718-b5e6432b9757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c6000b8-ce60-57f1-b233-3244b9f8994e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312219} :answer-field/id #uuid "41c09ba2-9813-5158-a120-598a3b499931" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e8515e5-bf34-50ba-8279-46c17d9b91fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-115940" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/92c557774a625d93c34fea181f435c1e6e544daa606a76c512bd96a7b9c047e7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(3-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-3)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/583fe0e13218462bdb4286ce456b0bcc4d43dd3772033e62a53242107a0a69db.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(3-y)$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-3)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=3,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0807fc1-d9f6-50cb-bb30-e9bf902a2a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312225} {:answer/id #uuid "71b97105-4333-5266-8968-c69b063df605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312226} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdd1474-0591-55f5-821c-feb3f22800b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312227} {:answer/id #uuid "24472044-ee31-5574-8a3d-7ce5819a1884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312228} {:answer/id #uuid "1aab3742-50fc-5bf6-8c8d-55931897464d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=y(y+1)(2-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24472044-ee31-5574-8a3d-7ce5819a1884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312228} :answer-field/id #uuid "a71dd1b3-b660-5833-8400-e07f3e2cdc68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8a58b057-32ea-5a42-a834-74970c5010b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-115941" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/9be53ddfc5bd7a471ced46b76f6c8bb78fbc8b6831cec1689b6d98e8561c0d87.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/50b53f35a936263f72a3962e32d841dc85519fb5ef3e7e138111c4efeb38137e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y+1)(2-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=y(y+1)(2-y).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb8be23-8455-52ab-868c-75a3ec81a649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312232} {:answer/id #uuid "fb950dbe-9671-55d1-b138-caa905e0fcaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312233} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ff1397-8951-572c-a3ae-31abc2d1a192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312234} {:answer/id #uuid "102a2155-0c38-564f-946d-62238737d750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312235} {:answer/id #uuid "d41e9604-0d94-5e4c-b65c-63080f31c6f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb950dbe-9671-55d1-b138-caa905e0fcaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312233} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbb1feca-29c3-579f-8d6c-bee8c2363c6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53ab2db1-0308-5084-926c-c3a100e39237" :question/math-academy-id "q-116038" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/f7000c9b2360173996e462883bdf9f89af674b9f099f25a866cd95bd286d9150.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4-{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-4}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/5f87023b47e66436ca4f6caa8e8cc651e7de80ab09e346d955e1bb12eea211b6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and positive for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4-{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to \\pm 2.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and  positive for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}}-1)}\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-4}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y\\in (-2,2)$ and positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1aa5239-8a35-5cd1-99ef-772d9e0a00f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312239} {:answer/id #uuid "1e2520e5-bba1-591b-8b1e-d69400ed3874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312240} {:answer/id #uuid "3f210340-3870-54dc-94c5-5a0a29010007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312241} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd18036-320b-5082-8f0f-9077e89d3c70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312242} {:answer/id #uuid "9479bf72-de58-51a0-af9c-b6beed775887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e2520e5-bba1-591b-8b1e-d69400ed3874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312240} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5c0ae67-1820-5b19-9dae-454cd1c16ee4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b18f97d9-f92d-5fde-89dd-212b5f96a0c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-116040" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/ddb340a99b8ab92d60e17184274854ce3d1b1bfa4972a79e74b8bfe9b0a81ce7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+3}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+3)^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{y+3}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/73ba54766cd08a0b596e43cd8da93209e2548c68821e70a44418a24dfdf27a38.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -3.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y<-3$ and negative for $y>-3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+3}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to -3.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y<-3$ and negative for $y>-3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{(y+3)^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for all $y\\ne -3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{y+3}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{y}{y+3}=-\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{3}{y})}\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e01615d7-3f77-5bc5-aa72-53fea87fc2a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312246} {:answer/id #uuid "53bd281b-bf8c-5d65-9736-ebf364853ef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312247} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd80012-7dc7-589b-a7aa-db23a84a638f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312248} {:answer/id #uuid "6514e6e8-9f9c-56a8-a1f3-a65db30f754e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312249} {:answer/id #uuid "129b7255-a50c-507e-919f-2ee16efe774c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cd80012-7dc7-589b-a7aa-db23a84a638f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312248} :answer-field/id #uuid "d04f0e4c-7667-5285-85f6-c703117462b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31f2f4de-cad5-594e-ac30-aec769441ed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-116150" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/158e4f6067ce12d5a31cc6ee0b217a8d11a459db5883fdd0b28ffdc2bd969a5e.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}+4{y}^{2}+4y$\n- ${y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/41b6dc7fd4e9a7dba6e14fe2d9fb87e7c07736ec0fc3cc111dd347b61c1075bb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}=-{y}^{2}(y+2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+4{y}^{2}+4y=y({y}^{2}+4y+4)=y(y+2)^{2}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}=y(2-y)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c25163c-f30b-5e16-88c0-91b7e09eb3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312253} {:answer/id #uuid "60bc6cbb-a2ef-516a-8ca2-6ccd95cb5c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312254} {:answer/id #uuid "11b57f26-8990-5ca2-9cc0-995ccf3e08ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312255} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6a98f0-ff1a-549c-bdc8-ba332d0a0a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=3{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312256} {:answer/id #uuid "7c953b27-c5e7-5fcc-897c-388b13d33867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=3y-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c25163c-f30b-5e16-88c0-91b7e09eb3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312253} :answer-field/id #uuid "7572c058-ad5a-5f9d-ad76-fe2d14b72320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b75bc494-1576-5354-8303-fbc7667847e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-116174" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/eeacce39a60a75fd71a77d54f0008f4469395d5e9b69912db8ce9896320b667f.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/dbb1d802854d4046479c69d297fe1807a9ded488409b385a6a2cffbf1196b4c4.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y=-y({y}^{2}-6y+9)=-y(y-3)^{2}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-3y=y(y-3).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y=-y(y-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d9044e2-97c2-5820-9088-65a28dff418a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312260} {:answer/id #uuid "dbfa02c7-833c-5efe-ba59-eb3b574ad385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312261} {:answer/id #uuid "08bfd063-6f2f-52b9-9627-c2d25cbfcdf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312262} {:answer/id #uuid "b3448238-3b73-56de-b9cd-7fccc0de471d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312263} {:answer/id #uuid "16b9f17b-75de-540c-9834-565e7d0a0a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{(y+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08bfd063-6f2f-52b9-9627-c2d25cbfcdf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312262} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd255b98-87ad-51ca-83be-5930aba26863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0cf6ca5-4b24-52a6-9510-ae0b22a2d68e" :question/math-academy-id "q-116177" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8f023d50d11e5b6604e5fcc257e35aa4b8615e8c91f5855d10e73293720c27e4.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/b6cf036f732d1cdff6f2e575b2ce082979d986416afd195282789303dcf70131.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}$ become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ do *not* become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d63798ff-0479-5d13-878a-735cc800f51c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312267} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc2ff0c-d522-53cc-ab4e-3e9e1dafd6be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312268} {:answer/id #uuid "adb0fa90-7eec-59c5-9b9e-f5011a5e0d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y-2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312269} {:answer/id #uuid "80497117-d1bc-5b5b-8215-5386df6cc0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312270} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2ca5bb-bd98-5421-8dbd-c8d6b5431cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea2ca5bb-bd98-5421-8dbd-c8d6b5431cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312271} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f547cd1-82bf-5b13-8e77-d0c062fd0344" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65e3360c-8bc5-5de3-ad51-6c40acc5b036" :question/math-academy-id "q-116179" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7b3aa65a62cb70ae297b57c5cfceecec8035afb621acec93d6fceabd1fd48729.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/890640809739a95a6d068f65678e124cc92d9e6d2fd86ec0380ee1f37a1c56d1.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 2$ only. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2-y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{(y-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the line $y=2$ divides the plane into $2$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $2$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-y}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cce3226c-452d-580a-9a03-de0b652a1869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312274} {:answer/id #uuid "43adfde6-2901-56c4-a0ad-7df55f56d1b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312275} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5127f1-fe51-5943-9856-7b1dd2716ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+4{y}^{2}+4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312276} {:answer/id #uuid "ebcca911-28ee-551b-a241-4543683394c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312277} {:answer/id #uuid "9d948788-8ca2-59d3-a4cf-35415dea2a4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=3{y}^{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebcca911-28ee-551b-a241-4543683394c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312277} :answer-field/id #uuid "90e75eb2-d763-5d94-9198-a9684c60f4fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db274ba7-dfb6-5f4f-a2db-8b2fa820f6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-116180" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/6d0a816057423c4f5397f876f775b0431eac550bb1a02d4e4f7021c33c37809e.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/f3b0dad3f1cb4b2216e3fce7f1fefa8beac15a6e16ab09ab3a4c47a2472e2f9a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=-3$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}={y}^{3}(y+3)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}={y}^{2}(y+3).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-3$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-3$ | $-3<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}={y}^{3}(y+3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "954f8004-35b4-58ec-be26-2beccfd5f8e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81024ebc-ba12-5903-a09f-3c9e98cbd8fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312281} {:answer/id #uuid "69e74a01-75a3-5cc9-bf84-43b063acb258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312282} {:answer/id #uuid "302e782b-387a-56e5-9141-4ebb721233e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312283} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2fee5c-e173-564f-a1ed-006c4ab030eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312284} {:answer/id #uuid "94c185cc-641f-5c64-9e4b-865f58373ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc2fee5c-e173-564f-a1ed-006c4ab030eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312284} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae1911b-7e1d-564c-96da-b734d45bfb63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbf5d076-ae90-5952-a22b-48de744a1f0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120106" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/76a0135eba2c33a3e69f9893d7c32a4cdc93e6d99825bbf6f72fb1db72b2a590.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(y-a)(y-b)^{2}.$ Find the value of $ab.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/68f424470a3a8ff98d7f738ce207c240795185e48ee527a434d04282d9023ed7.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=1$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-1)(y-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1,b=4,$ and $ab=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "954f8004-35b4-58ec-be26-2beccfd5f8e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fad63b7e-b8ff-59e9-85d5-0f4a555fb39b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312288} {:answer/id #uuid "a5df8253-1026-5c10-b261-19769cc09a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312289} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd60902-55f2-591f-92ac-5f908e97f2fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312290} {:answer/id #uuid "6639ee31-b828-535b-a581-8fbe92fc2a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312291} {:answer/id #uuid "78119c6e-9f30-5aa2-b51e-bae7da5d1f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dd60902-55f2-591f-92ac-5f908e97f2fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312290} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4004645-54fc-5341-a8f8-727e46b37ac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85f05352-e7af-5f82-b902-ef96f4b2f493" :question/math-academy-id "q-120109" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/3d24906da8cd181d40dbaec0095b8d51481babc152609fc378b03c61187220c3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7f8a042b9944b2cdf0e864b7dca760f094f4413f197befa05d6e5cbefb3841ba.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=1,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba43216d-5e4f-5b1a-ade8-53db1877cebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312295} {:answer/id #uuid "f77e5a48-7719-5664-a340-f3a7143e5697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312296} {:answer/id #uuid "e4528608-b8f6-52b6-a035-7eca4919a718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312297} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8a3618-c166-5d0d-a93e-60a1fc372873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312298} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b5720d-ff97-5461-9c63-37e9e90d56d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1b5720d-ff97-5461-9c63-37e9e90d56d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312299} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a745b38-cfed-5b6d-87e0-481970eae405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f68947f6-727a-5aaa-a57d-2f5ec5b7262d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120147" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/d27af23ca398eba62c2ab1fe2ee2cf9bfdf736c16fdd8408d2a13e0d34dae32c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y+2)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/ab076b61b443e031455f07c1e976467cc3b74651bfb5fc2fb39f89a74e156681.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y+2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41dad35d-a04d-5fcb-9983-3fd4fa201eab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312302} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6e0427-dc0d-538b-b8ab-d8f4c2cf984c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312303} {:answer/id #uuid "eba50d76-d9f5-5089-842e-07d51f06590b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312304} {:answer/id #uuid "a0215f4c-2fd6-556c-8c9b-52e5d10c12d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312305} {:answer/id #uuid "46ea7bde-54fb-53d9-94a0-2314d940ad54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eba50d76-d9f5-5089-842e-07d51f06590b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312304} :answer-field/id #uuid "d376dcd8-6ab3-5e00-965c-4c71cc887b0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1906b3f-2cb7-58b7-9f03-df08a78bc003" :question/math-academy-id "q-120176" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/440d321dd0eb1b5687b392aac95d5d7d36e5a31d4cb95d5397f88afe229d7aa6.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/0a8b34730f001c218b6c380b9f5af0ff4b2ffd8fbb295f82e4961c5242051986.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y=y(y-2)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3caef30d-9f08-5bde-a094-d108cdbdf078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312309} {:answer/id #uuid "84b25da4-79ef-567c-8b20-b9b54ac9b3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312310} {:answer/id #uuid "09652e71-b6a0-5e93-9146-201a70956aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312311} {:answer/id #uuid "67a3767d-f6cb-5912-9a81-6889c464a976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+2{y}^{2}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312312} {:answer/id #uuid "cef94c6b-a8cb-53c7-b44c-018dea98c37d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3caef30d-9f08-5bde-a094-d108cdbdf078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312309} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e9355f-771c-56cf-aeab-9931dd38b955" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "35f03faa-5461-5650-97e1-a210be6e8441" :question/math-academy-id "q-120177" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8d004b698e288cbfa3cab67b1744bd9bf40c018267fa1f44a633019f6f047a9a.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/895353a5359d43f8f6256453d4f97a4f35b33acd1241f0a2950b9b21f6313f50.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}=2{y}^{2}(y-2)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4}={y}^{3}(2-y).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}=2{y}^{2}(y-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}.$"}]}
